# AGENTS.md

This document defines the default ground rules for agents working under
`/KRAFTON/WORKSPACE/wbl-workspace/usrs/hyogon.ryu`.

## Scope

- The workspace root is `/KRAFTON/WORKSPACE/wbl-workspace/usrs/hyogon.ryu`.
- All work should stay inside this workspace unless the user explicitly approves an exception.
- Do not read, write, move, or delete files in sibling user workspaces.
- Do not modify shared account resources such as `~/.gitconfig`, `~/.ssh/`, or `~/.bashrc`.
- If external paths are needed for context, ask the user before touching them.

## Instruction Precedence

- This root `AGENTS.md` applies by default to all work under the workspace root.
- A project-local `AGENTS.md` may add more specific rules for that project.
- When rules conflict, the nearest `AGENTS.md` in the directory tree takes precedence.

## Language Policy

- Use English for code, filenames, commit messages, branch names, identifiers, and technical slugs.
- Respond to the user in Korean.
- Write project logs or memos in Korean when a project-specific rule requires them.
- Keep established English technical terms when translating would reduce clarity.

## Project Layout

- Keep only active projects and workspace-level support files at the workspace root.
- Start each new project in its own directory directly under the workspace root.
- Do not scatter temporary code, scripts, logs, or generated outputs directly in the root.
- Follow each project's own `AGENTS.md`, `README`, and existing structure.
- If a project has no local logging rule, create only the minimal structure needed for the task.

## Archive Policy

- Move completed, inactive, paused, or legacy projects under `archive/`.
- Treat files under `archive/` as read-only by default.
- Ask the user before modifying archived projects.
- Do not start new work inside `archive/`.
- Prefer moving projects into `archive/` over deleting them.

## Root Cleanup

- Before cleanup, identify which directories are active and which should be archived.
- Before moving a project to `archive/`, check its Git status and important untracked files.
- Do not archive unclear projects without user confirmation.
- After cleanup, verify that remaining root-level directories are active or intentionally shared.

## Git Identity

This workspace belongs to `hyogon.ryu`. Configure Git identity locally in every repository.

```bash
git config user.name "hyogon.ryu"
git config user.email "hyogon.ryu@krafton.com"
```

- Do not use `git config --global`.
- After cloning or creating a repository, set the local Git identity immediately.
- Before committing, verify the author identity with:

```bash
git config --local --get user.name
git config --local --get user.email
```

## Working Style

- Keep changes small and focused on one purpose at a time.
- Prefer the simplest implementation that solves the current task.
- Do not add premature abstractions, fallback paths, optional settings, or broad refactors.
- Run commands in readable units and inspect results before continuing.
- Keep documentation short and practical.
- Parallelize independent investigation or validation work when useful.
- Avoid parallel edits to the same files or the same code tree.
- Use separate worktrees when comparing multiple patch versions or running overlapping code changes.
- Prioritize traceability and clean separation over aggressive parallelism.
